  "Number": 14,
  "Question": "Given that \\(3^8 \\cdot 5^2 = a^b,\\) where both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive integers, find the smallest possible value for \\(a+b.\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(25\\)",
    "B": "\\(34\\)",
    "C": "\\(351\\)",
    "D": "\\(407\\)",
    "E": "\\(900\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Raising an integer to the \\(b\\)th power multiplies every exponent in its prime factorization by \\(b\\). Therefore, for \\(a^b=3^8\\cdot5^2\\), the positive integer \\(b\\) must divide both \\(8\\) and \\(2\\). This leaves exactly two possibilities.\n\nIf \\(b=1\\), then\n\\[\na+b=3^8\\cdot5^2+1=164026.\n\\]\nIf \\(b=2\\), then\n\\[\na=3^4\\cdot5=405,\n\\qquad a+b=405+2=407.\n\\]\nBoth possibilities satisfy the original equation, and the smaller sum is \\(\\boxed{407}\\), choice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
